From: da-ai-xian-zun <163512031+da-ai-xian-zun@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 02:18:07 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 03/10] selinux: never switch to enforcing at runtime

Clamp sel_write_enforce() so the global mode cannot be flipped to
enforcing after boot. ZUI's init in the Waydroid container writes
enforce=1 after loading its policy; the Debian host has no SELinux policy
and would lock up. The write still returns success so the container's init
does not abort. enforcing=0 on the built-in command line sets the initial
permissive mode, and enforcing_set() has only this and the boot-time
caller, so the guard is complete.

The kernel command line (CONFIG_CMDLINE, CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE) selects
lsm=selinux,landlock,lockdown,yama,loadpin,safesetid,bpf enforcing=0;
see kernel/configs/. AppArmor is therefore inactive on the host.
---
 security/selinux/selinuxfs.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/security/selinux/selinuxfs.c b/security/selinux/selinuxfs.c
index c7d914769..97eaf9d6b 100644
--- a/security/selinux/selinuxfs.c
+++ b/security/selinux/selinuxfs.c
@@ -155,6 +155,19 @@ static ssize_t sel_write_enforce(struct file *file, const char __user *buf,
 
 	new_value = !!scan_value;
 
+	/*
+	 * TB520FU Waydroid host-safety guard:
+	 * SELinux enforcing mode is global, not namespaced. ZUI Android init
+	 * in the container may write enforce=1 after loading its policy; that
+	 * would flip the shared mode to enforcing and lock the unlabeled Debian
+	 * host (which has no host SELinux policy). Clamp every write to
+	 * permissive so the real mode can never become enforcing. Boot cmdline
+	 * already sets enforcing=0; this pins it at runtime. The write still
+	 * returns success below so container init does not abort on setenforce.
+	 * Native Android uses its own GKI kernel and is unaffected.
+	 */
+	new_value = false;
+
 	old_value = enforcing_enabled();
 	if (new_value != old_value) {
 		length = avc_has_perm(current_sid(), SECINITSID_SECURITY,
